{"id": "sevt_015mNhc2Nkm8gMwqgjJ8Ndxu", "content": [{"text": "Confirmed. The per-row `order_items` queries are all in **checkout**, first appear at 14:38:22 (right after the 14:38:10 deploy), and climb from 24\u2192100+/min \u2014 the N+1 fanout.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- **Checkout p99**: flat ~75\u201380 ms through 14:37, jumps to 2201 ms @ 14:38, holds >4000 ms. Error rate stays 0 until 14:46 (DB saturation, not failures) \u2014 consistent with slow-not-broken.\n- **Deploys at the knee**: `de6541f` checkout @ 14:38:10, and `a9efec4` inventory @ 14:39:40.\n- **Timing rules out inventory**: p99 breaks at 14:38, *before* the inventory cache-disable deploy (14:39:40). The inventory change is a red herring here.\n- **`de6541f` diff**: the batched `OrderItem.objects.filter(order_id__in=ids)` + in-memory group was replaced with `OrderItem.objects.filter(order_id=order.order_id)` *inside the per-order loop* \u2014 a textbook N+1.\n- **Log confirms**: zero `SELECT * FROM order_items WHERE order_id = ?` queries before 14:38, then a burst (24 \u2192 85 \u2192 102 \u2192 115/min), all `service:checkout`, first at 14:38:22.\n\n---\n\n> **Root cause:** `de6541f` \u2014 the order-summary refactor turned one batched `order_items` query into a per-order query inside the loop (N+1), so checkout DB time and p99 blew up right after the 14:38:10 deploy.\n\nFix: revert `de6541f` (or restore the `order_id__in` batch fetch). The inventory cache toggle `a9efec4` is unrelated to this spike but worth watching separately.", "type": "text"}], "processed_at": "2026-10-14T05:30:11.454621Z", "type": "agent.message"}
